    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, a general from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afshar-tribe">Afshar tribe</a>, expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>'s occupiers and crowned himself Shah in 1736.</li><li>His 1739 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">sack of Delhi</a> crippled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>.</li><li>Built on one man's military genius, the empire shattered within months of his assassination in 1747.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The treasure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> took from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in 1739 was so vast that he remitted taxes throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> for three years.</p></aside>
    </section>

