    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shah.jpg" alt="Nader Shah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Contemporary_portrait_of_Nader_Shah._Artist_unknown,_created_in_ca._1740_in_Iran_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Afsharid Empire was founded in 1736 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>, one of history's most remarkable military commanders, who had risen from a modest background in the Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afshar-tribe">Afshar tribe</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khorasan">Khorasan</a> to become the most powerful man in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> before formally seizing the throne. Before proclaiming himself Shah, Nader, then known as Tahmasp Qoli Khan and serving as the leading general of the Safavid claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tahmasp-ii">Tahmasp II</a>, had already driven out all three foreign occupiers of Iranian territory: he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghilzai">Ghilzai Afghans</a> at Damghan and Murche-Khort and expelled them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 1729, liberating the Safavid capital after seven years of occupation; he pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> back in a series of western campaigns; and he induced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> to return its Caspian conquests through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-resht">Treaties of Resht</a> (1732) and Ganja (1735). By March 1736, when Nader set aside the infant Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbas-iii">Abbas III</a> and was proclaimed Shah after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughan-assembly">great assembly on the Mughan plain</a>, he commanded a large, battle-hardened army and had restored Iran's western and northern frontiers. The proclamation of the Afsharid dynasty was thus the formalisation of a military supremacy already achieved: Nader had restored Iranian territorial integrity and political coherence through years of warfare before assuming the crown. His legitimacy rested not on dynastic genealogy or religious sanction but almost entirely on military success, a foundation that would prove both the source of the empire's extraordinary power and the cause of its rapid collapse upon his death.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#muscat"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muscat.jpg" alt="Muscat" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#muscat">Muscat</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Alam_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tristan, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Afsharid economy was defined by the tension between vast military expenditure and the absence of a sustainable revenue system. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s incessant campaigns imposed crushing taxation on the Iranian population, and contemporary observers described provinces so exhausted that they could barely support his armies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Indian campaign</a> of 1738–1739 provided a temporary solution of spectacular proportions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">sack of Delhi</a> and the seizure of the Mughal imperial treasury yielded wealth that contemporaries estimated at hundreds of millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rupee">rupees</a>, so enormous that Nader remitted taxes throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> for three years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#koh-i-noor">Koh-i-Noor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#darya-i-noor">Darya-i-Noor</a> diamonds were among the prizes carried back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>, together with large numbers of artisans and craftsmen. This windfall, however, masked rather than resolved the underlying fiscal unsustainability of the Afsharid state. The empire had no developed commercial infrastructure independent of military extraction: trade routes that had functioned under Safavid patronage were disrupted by constant warfare, urban populations were impoverished by extraordinary tax demands, and the agricultural base of central and western Iran was damaged by the decades of conflict that preceded and followed Nader's accession. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bushehr">Bushehr</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bandar-abbas">Bandar Abbas</a> continued, and Nader's intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oman">Oman</a>, culminating in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muscat">Muscat</a> around 1743, extended Iranian reach into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>, but these revenues were insufficient to sustain an empire held together by military force.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shahs-invasion-of-india"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nader-shahs-invasion-of-india.jpg" alt="Nader Shah's invasion of India" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Nader Shah's invasion of India</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nadir_Shah_at_the_sack_of_Delhi_-_Battle_scene_with_Nader_Shah_on_horseback,_possibly_by_Muhammad_Ali_ibn_Abd_al-Bayg_ibn_Ali_Quli_Jabbadar,_mid-18th_century,_Museum_of_Fine_Arts,_Boston.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">possibly by Muhammad Ali ibn Abd al-Bayg ibn Ali Quli Jabbadar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s military achievements rank among the most remarkable in the history of warfare, and historians have often compared him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timur">Timur</a>. His army combined Persian, Turkic, Afghan, Kurdish and Georgian contingents under a unified command of exceptional discipline and tactical flexibility. His campaigns demonstrated mastery of every form of warfare: set-piece battles, above all Karnal (February 1739), where he crushed a Mughal army several times larger in a few hours; sieges; mountain warfare; naval operations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>; and strategic campaigning across vast distances. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shahs-invasion-of-india">Indian campaign</a> was perhaps his most spectacular achievement: forcing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khyber-pass">Khyber Pass</a> in late 1738, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peshawar">Peshawar</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indus">Indus</a>, occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lahore">Lahore</a> and, after Karnal, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#delhi">Delhi</a> in March 1739. In 1740 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, subduing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bukhara">Bukhara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khiva">Khiva</a>, while maintaining military pressure on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in the west, culminating in his victory at Kars in 1745. Nader made systematic use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#musket">musket</a>-armed troops, the <em>jazayerchis</em> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jezzailchi">jezzailchis</a>, and of mobile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">field artillery</a>, including swivel guns carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#camel">camels</a> (<em>zamburaks</em>), integrating firepower with his formidable cavalry. His naval programme, building a Persian Gulf fleet largely from scratch, showed ambitions that extended beyond land warfare. The army was nonetheless intensely personal: there was no professional officer corps or institutional training system independent of Nader himself.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#safavid-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/safavid-empire.jpg" alt="Safavid Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Safavid_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Safavid_Flag.png: Orange Tuesday (talk) The original uploader was Orange Tuesday at English Wikipedia. derivative wor..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Afsharid Empire possessed almost no governing infrastructure independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s personal authority. Unlike the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#safavid-empire">Safavid dynasty</a> he replaced, which had built a sophisticated administrative apparatus over more than two centuries, Nader governed through personal military command, officials answerable directly to him, and a system of taxation so intensive that it verged on the systematic spoliation of his own territories. There was no stable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizierate</a>, no codified administrative law and little bureaucratic continuity between the Safavid structures he inherited and the Afsharid state he created. Provincial government was typically assigned to military commanders, often kinsmen, fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afshar-tribe">Afshars</a> or trusted lieutenants from his campaigns, whose primary function was revenue extraction rather than civil administration. Nader attempted one significant institutional innovation: at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughan-assembly">Mughan assembly</a> in 1736 he proposed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#twelver-shiism">Twelver Shi'ism</a> be recognised as a fifth school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> jurisprudence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jafari-school">Ja'fari</a> <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#madhab">madhhab</a></em>, which would have positioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> as a bridge between Sunni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shia-islam-idea">Shi'a Islam</a> and eased the integration of his Sunni Afghan and Central Asian subjects. Although he pursued the idea through diplomacy and the religious conference at Najaf in 1743, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> never accepted it, the Shi'a clergy resented it, and it lapsed with his death. The growing suspicion and cruelty of Nader's later years, including the blinding of his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#reza-qoli">Reza Qoli</a> in 1742 on suspicion of plotting an assassination attempt, accelerated the decay of whatever administrative coherence the empire possessed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#peacock-throne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peacock-throne.jpg" alt="Peacock Throne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Mughal_Emperor_and_His_Court,_c._1774_-_V%26A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Afsharid Empire's impact on the political geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-asia">South Asia</a> was disproportionate to its brief span of eleven years as a great power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sack-of-delhi">sack of Delhi</a> in 1739 dealt a psychological and material blow to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> from which it never recovered: the loss of the imperial treasury undermined the financial foundations of Mughal authority, accelerated the empire's fragmentation into competing successor states, and helped create the power vacuum that the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#east-india-company">East India Company</a> and other European companies would eventually fill. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peacock-throne">Peacock Throne</a>, carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> and lost after Nader's death, became a symbol of the impoverishment of Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. His Central Asian campaigns temporarily reasserted Iranian influence over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khiva">Khiva</a> and the lands beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxus">Oxus</a>, regions that had long been outside Iranian political control. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> fleet and intervention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oman">Oman</a> demonstrated naval ambitions that later Iranian dynasties proved unable to sustain. The empire's collapse after 1747 created the conditions for the emergence of an independent Afghan state: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a>, formerly commander of Nader's Abdali Afghan contingent, was chosen as ruler at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kandahar">Kandahar</a> within months of Nader's death, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> that became the basis of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> itself, the Afsharid interlude was followed by half a century of division under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zand-dynasty">Zands</a> and rival claimants before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> reunified the country.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#erekle-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erekle-ii.jpg" alt="Erekle II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#erekle-ii">Erekle II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heraclius_II_of_Eastern_Georgia_crop.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UnknownUnknown derivative work: Carnby, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Afsharid Empire's decline was not gradual but abrupt, a collapse so swift that it stands as one of the most dramatic examples of an empire's dependence on a single ruler. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assassination-of-nader-shah">Nader Shah was assassinated</a> by his own officers on the night of 19–20 June 1747 at Fathabad near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quchan">Quchan</a>, after a plot involving commanders of his guard, including his own kinsmen, who had become convinced that his increasingly erratic and brutal behaviour threatened their lives. Disintegration began at once. Ahmad Khan Abdali, who had led Nader's Afghan contingent, withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kandahar">Kandahar</a> and was proclaimed Ahmad Shah later that year, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#durrani-empire">Durrani Empire</a> and taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and the eastern territories with him. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#georgia">Georgia</a>, Teimuraz II and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#erekle-ii">Erekle II</a> consolidated effective independence in Kartli and Kakheti, and the khanates of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caucasus">Caucasus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dagestan">Dagestan</a> threw off Persian control. In western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>, rival chieftains fought for power until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> emerged as master of most of the Iranian heartland. Within a few years the empire that had stretched from the Caucasus to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#indus">Indus</a> had contracted to a contested rump in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khorasan">Khorasan</a>. The civil war between Nader's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibrahim-shah">Ibrahim Shah</a> (1747–1748) reduced even this remnant to chaos. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shahrokh-shah">Shahrokh Shah</a>, Nader's grandson, blinded in 1750, survived as a nominal ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mashhad">Mashhad</a> until 1796, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agha-mohammad-khan-qajar">Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar</a> took the city and had Shahrokh tortured to reveal the remaining Afsharid treasures; he died of his injuries, and the dynasty effectively came to an end. The Afsharid trajectory, from obscurity to one of the most powerful states of its age within a decade and from that peak to fragmentation almost overnight, has few parallels in history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Afsharid Empire stands as history's most extreme example of the one-man state: an empire that rose from nothing to the first rank of world powers in little more than a decade and fragmented almost immediately after its founder's death, leaving behind few institutions, no effective successor and little continuity, only the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>.</em></p>

